neer.\nGenerate a property-based testing harness for the following PyTorch reference solution in module \'NumPy Basics & Data Structures\'.\n\n--- DOMAIN CONTRACTS ---\nTarget Input Shape: [batch_size, num_features]\nTarget Output Shape: [batch_size, num_classes]\n\n--- REFERENCE SOLUTION CODE ---\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nfrom torchvision.models import vit_b_16, ViT_B_16_Weights\n\nclass CustomViT(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super(CustomViT, self).__init__()\n        # Load the vit_b_16 model with ImageNet weights\n        self.vit = vit_b_16(weights=ViT_B_16_Weights.IMAGENET1K_V1)\n        # Replace the final fully connected layer to output 10 classes\n        num_ftrs = self.vit.heads.head.in_features\n        self.vit.heads.head = nn.Linear(num_ftrs, 10)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        # Forward pass through the network\n        return self.vit(x)\n\n# Example usage (no need to modify)\nif __name__ == \'__main__\':\n    model = CustomViT()\n    x = torch.randn(4, 3, 224, 224)  # Batch of 4 images, each 224x224 pixels, 3 color channels\n    output = model(x)\n    print(output.shape)  # Should print [4, 10]\n\nQA HARNESS REQUIREMENTS:\n1. SOLUTION IMPORT: Include `from numpy_basics_solution import *` at the top of `unit_test` so tests can import solution classes/functions when executed standalone.\n2. PROGRAMMATIC MULTI-SHAPE FUZZING: In `unit_test`, iterate over a small loop of varying batch sizes (e.g. `for batch_size in [2, 4]:`) to verify model output shapes remain dynamically resilient.\n3. PROPERTY-BASED INVARIANT HARNESSING: Do NOT rely solely on trivial assertions. Assert mathematical invariants:\n   a. Shape Contracts: Assert exact output tensor shapes across varying batch sizes.\n   b. Numerical & Gradient Integrity: Assert no `NaN` or `Inf` values exist in output or gradients (`assert not torch.isnan(output).any()`).\n   c. Value Bounds: Assert output ranges match mathematical expectations (e.g. Softmax sums to 1.0, S